From 829adcbd7e1d6e109ae2cd81f67f4e46a84f5c95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JuggyMcNutty <11588877+JuggyMcNutty@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 09:40:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] VM: calls without heap allocations or walks over every local

FORK ONLY. This project ships a NO-AI Code Rule asking that LLM-written
changes stay in a fork and are never PR'd upstream. These were written with
Claude. Do not submit them.

Frame::Call was the costliest VM function on the handheld after the
evaluator itself (3.6 ms of the game tick's self time a frame), for work
that does not depend on the call:

- Parameters: Frame::Call, CallScript and CallNative each walked the
  function's Properties -- every local, a cache miss per property object
  on the Cortex-A53 -- to find its parameters, three or four walks a call.
  UFunction::CallParms keeps the Parm properties in order and ReturnParm
  the return value, gathered at the first call. A temporary sweep of all
  7,673 functions loaded on Liberty Island found every return value also
  a Parm and none with two, so the two lists say what the walks did.
- Arguments: an Array on the heap, grown again when a native's return
  value was appended. CallArguments keeps up to eight on the stack, with
  room made up front for what Frame::Call appends (CallArguments::Room);
  the Array overload of Frame::Call, for native callers, moves into one.
- Locals: a LocalVariables allocated for each script call, and its data
  allocated again. The frame holds its LocalVariables, whose data lives in
  a 256-byte buffer inside it when the function's locals fit.
- Disabled events: every call looked up the object's state name and its
  disabled set, though almost no object disables anything. The lookups
  are skipped when DisabledEvents is empty, and EnableEvent now removes a
  state's entry when its set empties, so that stays true.

Liberty Island's log is the same as before.
